      "id": 1,
      "text": "Renamed Viola on December 26, 1882—likely in honor of the daughter of the first postmaster, William H.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/viola_idaho",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Spokane and Inland Empire Railroad arrived in the early 1900s, turning Viola into a passenger station that shipped apples across the Inland Northwest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/viola_idaho",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Viola Cemetery, founded in 1896 on a hill overlooking the town, holds more than 750 graves of early settlers and veterans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/viola_idaho",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Viola sits in the Palouse, where rolling loess hills make the land perfect for dryland wheat and legume farming.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/viola_idaho",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Viola has a humid continental climate, with cold winters, warm summers, and most rain falling in fall and winter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/viola_idaho",
